(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pleural-disorders-quiz"]={"title":"Pleural Disorders Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does the visceral pleural line of a pneumothorax appear on a chest radiograph?","choices":["Flat, with fluid beneath it","Thin, convex toward the chest wall","Concave toward the chest wall","Thick, with markings beyond it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is established by the visceral pleural line, the interface of lung and pleural air: thin, sharp lines that are convex outward toward the chest wall. Bronchovascular markings are not typically visible beyond that edge. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 34, 71"},{"q":"Below what volume does a pleural effusion produce no physical exam findings?","choices":["10 mL","75 mL","300 mL","1000 mL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Small effusions, under 300 mL, produce no physical exam findings. Larger ones bring asymmetrical expansion, decreased tactile fremitus, dullness and diminished breath sounds, and more than 1000 mL can shift the trachea away from the involved side. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 87, 90, 93"},{"q":"In Light's criteria, which pleural-to-serum protein ratio marks an exudate?","choices":["Less than 0.5","Greater than 2/3","Greater than 0.5","Greater than 0.6"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The protein criterion is pleural fluid protein divided by serum protein greater than 0.5. An exudate is high-protein fluid that leaks out through increased vascular permeability from inflammation, and meeting this criterion alone is enough. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"Which describes chemical rather than mechanical pleurodesis?","choices":["Instilled talc or sclerosant","Manual abrasion of the pleura","Stapling of apical blebs","Air removed by needle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chemical pleurodesis is instillation of a sclerosant, drugs or talc, into the pleural space, performed via a chest tube or thoracoscopically. Mechanical pleurodesis is manual abrasion of the pleura performed via surgical intervention. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 46, 47, 57"},{"q":"Which symptom is less commonly observed in primary spontaneous pneumothorax?","choices":["Cough","Acute onset dyspnea","Ipsilateral pleuritic pain","Fatigue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cough is less commonly observed in primary spontaneous pneumothorax. The usual picture is acute onset dyspnea with ipsilateral acute pleuritic chest pain, a severe stabbing pain that increases with deep breathing; a small one may be asymptomatic or cause only fatigue. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Besides treating the underlying condition, which treatment is listed for pleurisy?","choices":["Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatories","Broad-spectrum antibiotics","Chemical pleurodesis","Therapeutic thoracentesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pleurisy is treated by treating the underlying condition and with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for the inflamed parietal pleura. Diagnostic testing likewise depends on the underlying condition, because pleurisy is a symptom rather than a diagnosis. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 14, 45, 46, 96"},{"q":"A first primary spontaneous pneumothorax has an apex-to-cupola distance under 3 cm and causes minimal symptoms. Which treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Thoracoscopic pleurodesis","Observation with oxygen","Chest tube insertion","Catheter aspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A small primary spontaneous pneumothorax, under 3 cm at the apex, with no or minimal symptoms is observed with adequate analgesia and 100% oxygen by non-rebreather mask, which accelerates resorption of air. A repeat film in 6 to 12 hours confirms it has not expanded before discharge. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 55"},{"q":"What benefit does open thoracotomy offer in a complicated pneumothorax?","choices":["No general anesthesia","Lysis of previous adhesions","Less patient discomfort","Lower overall cost"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thoracoscopic surgery has supplanted thoracotomy, but open thoracotomy remains valuable for complicated cases: it allows examination of the lung for the site of the air leak and enables lysis of previous adhesions. Its drawbacks are anesthesia risk, increased cost and significant discomfort. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 50"},{"q":"What does an arterial blood gas usually show in pneumothorax?","choices":["Hypoxemia, respiratory alkalosis","Hypoxemia, respiratory acidosis","Normal oxygen, metabolic acidosis","Hypercapnia, metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An arterial blood gas usually reveals hypoxemia and respiratory alkalosis, though results depend on ventilation status and the compensatory response. The test is often unnecessary and is kept for severe underlying lung disease or persistent respiratory distress despite treatment. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which feature belongs to the visceral pleura rather than the parietal pleura?","choices":["Intercostal nerve fibers","Higher hydrostatic pressure","No sensory innervation","Systemic blood supply"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The visceral pleura covers the lungs, takes its blood from the pulmonary circulation, has the lower hydrostatic pressure and has no sensory innervation. That is why pleuritic pain comes from the parietal layer, whose pain fibers are stimulated when the two layers rub together. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9"},{"q":"Which pair of physical findings is expected over a pneumothorax?","choices":["Hyperresonance, decreased fremitus","Dullness, decreased fremitus","Hyperresonance, increased fremitus","Dullness, increased fremitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Over a pneumothorax, percussion is hyperresonant and tactile fremitus is decreased or absent, because pleural air impedes the transmission of vibrations from the larynx to the chest surface. Breath and voice sounds are diminished or absent on the affected side as well. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 87, 88"},{"q":"What usually causes pleurodynia?","choices":["Bacterial pneumonia","Coxsackie B virus","Herpes zoster","Tuberculosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pleurodynia is a viral myalgia, often caused by coxsackie B virus, an enterovirus, and typically follows an upper respiratory or gastrointestinal infection. Also called Bornholm disease, it peaks in summer and spreads mainly by the fecal-oral route. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 103, 107, 116, 118, 119"},{"q":"By which mechanism does heart failure cause a pleural effusion?","choices":["Lowered oncotic pressure","Raised capillary permeability","Blocked lymphatic drainage","Raised hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure causes effusion through increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, which moves fluid across a membrane and produces a low-protein transudate. Normally fluid enters the pleural space from parietal pleural capillaries and is removed via parietal pleural lymphatics. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 110"},{"q":"Which examination finding separates a pleural effusion from a pneumothorax?","choices":["Decreased tactile fremitus","Diminished breath sounds","Hyperresonance","Dullness to percussion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Percussion separates them: an effusion is dull over the fluid, while a pneumothorax is hyperresonant. Both decrease tactile fremitus and diminish breath sounds on the affected side, because fluid and air each obstruct the transmission of vibrations and sound. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 87, 88"},{"q":"What advantage does a Heimlich valve offer a patient with a pneumothorax?","choices":["It allows two-way air flow","It promotes ambulation","It prevents recurrence","It seals the air leak"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A Heimlich valve is a one-way flutter valve attached to a tube inserted in the chest, open to air or attached to a drainage bag. The device is small and portable, which promotes ambulation; it is listed with chest tube insertion for a small primary pneumothorax with significant dyspnea. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 40, 45, 49, 54, 59"},{"q":"Which complication can follow a retained hemothorax?","choices":["Pneumomediastinum","Pleurodynia","Empyema","Chylothorax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Undrained blood collections serve as a primary nidus for infection, and a retained hemothorax directly leads to empyema, fibrothorax and trapped lung. That is why chest tube placement is the essential first step for drainage. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 74, 75, 105, 116"},{"q":"How is a spontaneous pneumothorax classified in a tall, thin patient with Marfan syndrome?","choices":["Primary spontaneous","Traumatic","Iatrogenic","Secondary spontaneous"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Marfan syndrome is a connective tissue disease, and connective tissue disease is listed among the underlying abnormalities of secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. The tall, thin build fits the primary profile, but the underlying disorder makes the event secondary. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 19, 20"},{"q":"How many of Light's criteria must a pleural fluid meet to be classified as an exudate?","choices":["The protein criterion","At least one","At least two","Each of the three"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An exudate meets at least one of Light's criteria: pleural-to-serum protein ratio above 0.5, pleural-to-serum lactate dehydrogenase ratio above 0.6, or pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase above two-thirds of the upper limit of normal serum lactate dehydrogenase. A transudate meets none of them. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"Where is the needle placed for needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax?","choices":["Second intercostal space, midaxillary line","Second intercostal space, opposite side","Under the third rib, midclavicular line","Second intercostal space, midclavicular line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The typical needle decompression site is on the thoracic side of the pneumothorax, in the 2nd intercostal space, over the 3rd rib, at the midclavicular line. Patient body habitus can change the choice, which is why this is called the typical site. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 64, 67, 68"},{"q":"Which pair of findings fits a tension pneumothorax?","choices":["Hypotension and neck emphysema","Bradycardia and flat neck veins","Hypotension and tracheal deviation","Hypertension and tracheal deviation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a tension pneumothorax the mediastinum shifts to the contralateral side, venous return and cardiac output fall, and the patient shows hypotension, tracheal deviation and neck vein distention, with decreased or absent breath sounds. Those bedside signs are enough to act on. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 64, 77"},{"q":"When counseling patients after a spontaneous pneumothorax, which group needs early surgical evaluation for safety?","choices":["Office workers","Long-distance runners","Former smokers","Pilots and divers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pilots and divers need early surgical options for safety: high-pressure activities such as diving carry severe recurrence dangers, and scuba diving is to be avoided. Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery closes leaks to prevent future collapses. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 59, 60"},{"q":"What is the initial imaging test for a suspected pneumothorax in a stable patient?","choices":["Supine chest radiograph","Arterial blood gas","Upright chest radiograph","Chest computed tomography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The initial test for pneumothorax is a standard upright chest radiograph, which establishes the diagnosis by showing the visceral pleural line. It serves as rapid initial triage for the upright, symptomatic patient; computed tomography is the gold standard but is not the first test. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 29, 33, 34, 35"},{"q":"After needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax, which step follows?","choices":["Observation with oxygen","Catheter aspiration","Chemical pleurodesis","Chest tube insertion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Needle decompression is followed by moderate or large-size chest tube insertion and admission; immediate chest tube placement would have been ideal had a tube been readily available. The needle relieves the tension, and the chest tube then drains the pleural space. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 53, 57, 66"},{"q":"About how much fluid does the normal pleural cavity contain?","choices":["About 10 mL","About 75 mL","About 300 mL","About 1000 mL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pleural cavity is a potential space that contains about 10 mL of fluid, roughly 2 teaspoons. That thin film lubricates the layers and maintains coupling of the lung with the chest wall; fluid inflow from the parietal capillaries equals reabsorption by the parietal lymphatics. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 5, 87, 90"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of chylothorax?","choices":["Trauma such as thoracic surgery","Tumor in the mediastinum","Bacterial pneumonia","Heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chylothorax is thoracic duct disruption that lets lipid from chyle, lymph fluid, collect in the pleural space; trauma, for example thoracic surgery, is the most common cause, and tumors in the mediastinum are the other. Without obvious trauma, a lymphangiogram and computed tomography assess the mediastinum. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 82, 104, 105, 107"},{"q":"A secondary spontaneous pneumothorax needs recurrence prevention, but surgery is not an option. What is used instead?","choices":["Observation with oxygen","Chemical pleurodesis via chest tube","Open thoracotomy with abrasion","Repeat catheter aspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most secondary spontaneous pneumothoraces are treated with a chest tube and may go on to thoracoscopic surgery or thoracotomy with bleb stapling and pleural abrasion; if surgery is not an option, chemical pleurodesis is performed via the chest tube. The sclerosant obliterates the space without an operation. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 46, 49, 57"},{"q":"What causes a pleural effusion to become loculated?","choices":["Adhesions between pleural layers","Raised hydrostatic pressure","Thoracic duct disruption","Air trapped above the fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loculations are fluid-filled compartments within the pleural space that occur when there are adhesions between the visceral and parietal pleurae. They go with conditions that cause pleural inflammation, such as empyema, and the fluid stays stuck in a pocket instead of layering freely. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 71, 82, 92, 94, 104"},{"q":"Which history can precede a spontaneous pneumomediastinum?","choices":["Heart failure","Violent coughing","Coxsackie B infection","Thoracic duct injury"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spontaneous pneumomediastinum may follow a sudden increase in intrathoracic pressure, such as violent coughing, vomiting or inhalation drug use with a Valsalva maneuver. Excessive intra-alveolar pressure ruptures alveoli bordering the mediastinum, and air collects in the mediastinal tissues. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 75, 76, 82, 104, 116"},{"q":"Which option lets a patient with a short prognosis and a recurrent malignant effusion drain fluid at home?","choices":["Repeated inpatient thoracentesis","Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery","Indwelling tunneled pleural catheter","Chemical pleurodesis via chest tube"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An indwelling tunneled pleural catheter is placed in the outpatient setting and intermittently drained at home, which minimizes time in hospital for patients with short prognoses. It runs through a one-way valve to a negative-pressure bottle. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 49, 96, 97, 113, 114"},{"q":"Where is a chest tube most commonly inserted?","choices":["2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line","4th or 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line","2nd intercostal space, midaxillary line","4th or 5th intercostal space, midaxillary line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The most common chest tube site is the fourth or fifth intercostal space at the midaxillary line. Chest tube thoracostomy places the tube to drain fluid or air from the pleural space, for pneumothorax or pleural effusion. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 41, 67, 68, 73"}]};
